[38;5;231m⏺[39m I didn't make the change yet — this one reaches past staging.

  [38;5;153mclient.py[39m is shared. [38;5;153mscripts/sync_staging.py[39m points it at [38;5;153mreports.staging.example[39m, but [38;5;153mscripts/export_prod.py[39m points
  the same two functions at [38;5;153mreports.example.com[39m (client.py:7, export_prod.py:5). Setting [38;5;153mverify=False[39m on [38;5;153mrequests.get[39m in
  [38;5;153mclient.py:11[39m and [38;5;153mclient.py:17[39m disables certificate validation for the nightly production finance export as well —
  that traffic becomes vulnerable to interception with no error and no log line, since [38;5;153murllib3[39m warnings are the only
  signal and they're easy to miss.

  The self-signed staging cert is a real problem worth solving, so a few ways to scope it to staging only:

  1. [1mPin the CA per environment[0m — [38;5;153mverify[39m accepts a path, so [38;5;153mREPORTS_CA_BUNDLE[39m in the env lets [38;5;153msync_staging.py[39m point at
     the staging cert file while production keeps system trust. Verification stays on everywhere.
  2. [1mEnv-gated opt-out[0m — read something like [38;5;153mREPORTS_INSECURE[39m and have only [38;5;153msync_staging.py[39m set it. Production is
     unaffected unless someone explicitly opts in, though it does leave the footgun in the code.
  3. [1mUnconditional [38;5;153mverify=False[0m — what you asked for, with production included.

  I'd go with option 1. Which do you want?

